= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

On a smooth <wavefunction> $f$, the <position operator> and <momentum operator> give
$$
[X,P]f=-i\hbar xf'+i\hbar(xf)'=i\hbar f.
$$
Thus the <canonical commutation relation> is $\boxed{[X,P]=i\hbar I}$. Since $X$ and $P$ are <self-adjoint operators> and $m,\omega$ are real, the <adjoint operator> of $a_-$ is
$$
\boxed{a_+=P+im\omega X.}
$$
Expanding the products in the stated order is essential:
$$
\begin{aligned}
a_+a_-&=P^2+m^2\omega^2X^2+im\omega[X,P]=2mH-m\hbar\omega,\\
a_-a_+&=P^2+m^2\omega^2X^2-im\omega[X,P]=2mH+m\hbar\omega.
\end{aligned}
$$
Subtracting gives $\boxed{[a_-,a_+]=2m\hbar\omega I}$. These identities initially hold on smooth rapidly decaying functions, which contain the <quantum harmonic oscillator> <energy eigenstates> used below.

The factorization also proves a lower bound: for a normalized <energy eigenstate> $H\psi=E\psi$,
$$
0\leq\|a_-\psi\|^2=\langle\psi,a_+a_-\psi\rangle=2m\left(E-\frac12\hbar\omega\right).
$$
Therefore $E\geq E_0:=\hbar\omega/2$. The <commutator product rule>, applied to $H=a_+a_-/(2m)+E_0$, gives
$$
[H,a_+]=\hbar\omega a_+,\qquad [H,a_-]=-\hbar\omega a_-.
$$
So a nonzero application of the <creation operator> $a_+$ raises the <energy eigenvalue> by $\hbar\omega$, while the <annihilation operator> $a_-$ lowers it by the same amount.

Begin with any <energy eigenstate>, whose existence is assumed. Repeated lowering must terminate: otherwise it would eventually produce a nonzero <energy eigenstate> below $E_0$, contradicting the lower bound. More explicitly, if $(E-E_0)/(\hbar\omega)=N+r$ with integer $N\geq0$ and $0\leq r<1$, the first $N$ lowering steps are nonzero. If $r>0$, one further step is also nonzero by its positive squared <norm> and has energy $E_0+(r-1)\hbar\omega<E_0$, an impossibility. Thus $r=0$ and the last state satisfies $a_-\psi_0=0$. This proves
$$
\boxed{E_n=\left(n+\frac12\right)\hbar\omega,\qquad n=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$
Every level exists as well: $\|a_+\psi_n\|^2=2m(E_n+\hbar\omega/2)=2m\hbar\omega(n+1)>0$, so repeated raising from $\psi_0$ never vanishes. The same factorization bounds the <energy expectation> of any normalized state in the <one-dimensional harmonic oscillator form domain> below by $E_0$, proving that the terminated state really is a <ground state>.

To find its <probability density>, solve the first-order equation
$$
(P-im\omega X)\psi_0=-i\left(\hbar\psi_0'+m\omega x\psi_0\right)=0.
$$
Its solutions are $\psi_0=C_0\exp[-m\omega x^2/(2\hbar)]$. With $\alpha=m\omega/\hbar>0$, the supplied <Gaussian integral> gives
$$
1=|C_0|^2\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-\alpha x^2}\,dx=|C_0|^2\sqrt{\frac\pi\alpha}.
$$
Taking $C_0=(\alpha/\pi)^{1/4}$ fixes an irrelevant <global phase>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{|\psi_0(x)|^2=\sqrt{\frac{m\omega}{\pi\hbar}}\exp\left(-\frac{m\omega x^2}{\hbar}\right).}
$$
The <ground-state energy> is positive even though the potential minimum is zero: it is the oscillator's <zero-point energy>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $\gamma=2m\hbar\omega$. If $\psi_n$ is a normalized <energy eigenstate> at $E_n$, the factorization from part (a) gives
$$
\|a_+\psi_n\|^2=\langle\psi_n,a_-a_+\psi_n\rangle=\gamma(n+1).
$$
It follows inductively that $v_n=(a_+)^n\psi_0$ has squared <norm> $\gamma^n n!$. The <commutator> with $H$ establishes its <energy eigenvalue> at each step. Hence
$$
\boxed{\psi_n=\frac{(a_+)^n\psi_0}{(2m\hbar\omega)^{n/2}\sqrt{n!}}}
$$
is normalized and has $E_n=(n+\tfrac12)\hbar\omega$. Distinct levels are <orthogonal> because $H$ is a <self-adjoint operator>. In particular,
$$
a_+\psi_n=\sqrt{\gamma(n+1)}\,\psi_{n+1},\qquad a_-\psi_n=\sqrt{\gamma n}\,\psi_{n-1}.
$$

To decompose the specified initial <wavefunction>, retain this exact phase convention for the <creation and annihilation operators>. Their difference gives
$$
X=\frac{a_+-a_-}{2im\omega}.
$$
Since $a_-\psi_0=0$ and $a_-a_+\psi_0=\gamma\psi_0$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
X^2\psi_0&=-\frac1{4m^2\omega^2}(a_+^2-a_+a_--a_-a_++a_-^2)\psi_0\\
&=\frac{\hbar}{2m\omega}\left(\psi_0-\sqrt2\,\psi_2\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
This <quadratic excitation of an oscillator ground state>, $\Psi(x,0)=C_0^{-1}X^2\psi_0$, contains only the $n=0$ and $n=2$ <energy eigenstates>. The printed initial function has an unspecified overall normalization, so the <Born rule> requires division by its squared <norm>. After <wavefunction normalization> the state is
$$
\frac1{\sqrt3}\psi_0-\sqrt{\frac23}\psi_2.
$$
The minus sign is consistent with the paper's convention: its $a_+$ is $i$ times the usual real-coordinate raising operator multiplied by $\sqrt\gamma$, so its $\psi_2$ has the opposite sign to the usual real second <harmonic oscillator eigenfunction>. The <energy measurement> probabilities are independent of this choice of phase.

Therefore the complete answer is
$$
\boxed{\Pr\left(E=\frac12\hbar\omega\right)=\frac13,\qquad \Pr\left(E=\frac52\hbar\omega\right)=\frac23.}
$$
**Every other energy has probability zero.** At any later time the two coefficients only acquire their separate <dynamical phases>, so these <probabilities> remain the same. A direct check of the normalization is
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x^4e^{-\alpha x^2}\,dx=\frac{d^2}{d\alpha^2}\sqrt{\frac\pi\alpha}=\frac{3\sqrt\pi}{4\alpha^{5/2}},\qquad \alpha=\frac{m\omega}{\hbar}.
$$
This also confirms the factor of three in the squared <norm> of the two-component expansion.

= 3
{parent=2017}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The given <angular momentum commutation relations> imply $[L_3,L_1]=i\hbar L_2$ and $[L_3,L_2]=-i\hbar L_1$. By <linearity>,
$$
[L_3,L_\pm]=i\hbar L_2\pm i(-i\hbar L_1)=\boxed{\pm\hbar L_\pm}.
$$
Similarly,
$$
[L_+,L_-]=-2i[L_1,L_2]=\boxed{2\hbar L_3}.
$$
For the remaining <commutators>, the <commutator product rule> gives, for each $j$,
$$
[L^2,L_j]=\sum_i\left(L_i[L_i,L_j]+[L_i,L_j]L_i\right)=i\hbar\sum_{i,k}\epsilon_{ijk}(L_iL_k+L_kL_i)=0.
$$
The last sum vanishes because the <Levi-Civita symbol> is antisymmetric under $i\leftrightarrow k$, while $L_iL_k+L_kL_i$ is symmetric. Taking $j=3$ and then linear combinations with $j=1,2$ proves
$$
\boxed{[L^2,L_3]=0=[L^2,L_\pm].}
$$

If $L^2\psi=\lambda\hbar^2\psi$ and $L_3\psi=m\hbar\psi$, these identities yield
$$
L^2L_+\psi=L_+L^2\psi=\lambda\hbar^2L_+\psi,\qquad L_3L_+\psi=(L_+L_3+\hbar L_+)\psi=(m+1)\hbar L_+\psi.
$$
One must also prove that this candidate <eigenstate> is nonzero. Since the <angular momentum operators> are <self-adjoint operators>, $L_+^*=L_-$, and expanding the product gives
$$
L_-L_+=L_1^2+L_2^2+i[L_1,L_2]=L^2-L_3^2-\hbar L_3.
$$
Therefore
$$
\|L_+\psi\|^2=\hbar^2\bigl[\lambda-m(m+1)\bigr]\|\psi\|^2.
$$
This is nonnegative and, by the assumed inequality, nonzero. Hence it is positive. The <angular momentum raising operator> really does produce a nonzero simultaneous <eigenstate> with the claimed <eigenvalues>.

To prove that raising terminates, let $v$ be any nonzero state reached after $r$ steps, with projection $q=m+r$. Positivity gives
$$
\lambda\hbar^2\|v\|^2=\sum_{i=1}^3\|L_iv\|^2\geq\|L_3v\|^2=q^2\hbar^2\|v\|^2,
$$
so $q^2\leq\lambda$. Thus the sequence cannot remain nonzero for every integer $r\geq0$. At its last nonzero state $v=(L_+)^k\psi$, one has $L_+v=0$, and the squared <norm> identity gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda=(m+k)(m+k+1)\quad\text{for some integer }k\geq0.}
$$
Under the paper's initial inequality the first step is nonzero, so in fact $k\geq1$. An <angular momentum ladder endpoint> must therefore be reached: bounded projection follows from positivity, and the final raising <norm> fixes the total <angular momentum> <eigenvalue>. Writing $j=m+k$, the complementary product $L_+L_-=L^2-L_3^2+\hbar L_3$ gives $\|L_-v\|^2=2j\hbar^2\|v\|^2$, also showing $j\geq0$.

