{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Put $f_l(\theta,\phi)=\sin^l\theta\,e^{-il\phi}$. For the regular, single-valued <orbital angular momentum> states, $l$ is a nonnegative integer. Differentiation immediately gives
$$
L_3f_l=-i\hbar\partial_\phi f_l=(-i\hbar)(-il)f_l=\boxed{-l\hbar f_l}.
$$
For $0<\theta<\pi$, the derivatives needed for $L^2$ are
$$
\partial_\theta f_l=l\cot\theta f_l,\qquad \partial_\theta^2f_l=(l^2\cot^2\theta-l\csc^2\theta)f_l,\qquad \partial_\phi^2f_l=-l^2f_l.
$$
Consequently the angular differential expression inside the parentheses of $-L^2/\hbar^2$ equals
$$
\left[(l^2+l)\cot^2\theta-(l+l^2)\csc^2\theta\right]f_l=-l(l+1)f_l,
$$
using $\csc^2\theta-\cot^2\theta=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{L^2f_l=l(l+1)\hbar^2f_l.}
$$
The expressions extend regularly through the polar coordinate singularities: $f_l$ is the restriction of the polynomial $(x-iy)^l$ to the unit sphere. It is proportional to the lowest-projection <spherical harmonic> of degree $l$; for $l=0$ it is the constant state.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Starting from $f_{-1}=\sin\theta\,e^{-i\phi}$, part (i) gives $L^2f_{-1}=2\hbar^2f_{-1}$ and $L_3f_{-1}=-\hbar f_{-1}$. Apply the <angular momentum raising operator> explicitly:
$$
L_+f_{-1}=\hbar e^{i\phi}\left(\cos\theta e^{-i\phi}+i\cot\theta(-i\sin\theta e^{-i\phi})\right)=2\hbar\cos\theta.
$$
A second application gives
$$
L_+\cos\theta=-\hbar\sin\theta e^{i\phi}.
$$
Since $L_+$ commutes with $L^2$, both raised functions retain the <eigenvalue> $2\hbar^2$. Their $L_3$ <eigenvalues> follow either by differentiating their $\phi$ dependence or from $[L_3,L_+]=\hbar L_+$. Thus three valid unnormalized functions, in the requested order, are
$$
\boxed{f_{-1}=\sin\theta e^{-i\phi},\qquad f_0=\cos\theta,\qquad f_{+1}=\sin\theta e^{i\phi}.}
$$
For later <Born rule> calculations, normalize with the <solid angle> measure $d\Omega=\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi$. Their squared <norms> are $8\pi/3$, $4\pi/3$, $8\pi/3$, respectively. For example $\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\,d\theta=4/3$ and $\int_0^\pi\cos^2\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta=2/3$. A convenient <orthonormal basis> is therefore
$$
\chi_{-1}=\sqrt{\frac3{8\pi}}\sin\theta e^{-i\phi},\qquad \chi_0=\sqrt{\frac3{4\pi}}\cos\theta,\qquad \chi_{+1}=\sqrt{\frac3{8\pi}}\sin\theta e^{i\phi}.
$$
<Orthogonality> follows from the distinct Fourier factors in $\phi$. An overall phase of each basis function is arbitrary; these choices need not impose the usual phase convention for the <spherical harmonics>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Recover $L_1$ from the given <angular momentum raising operator> and <angular momentum lowering operator>:
$$
L_1=\frac12(L_++L_-)=i\hbar\left(\sin\phi\,\partial_\theta+\cot\theta\cos\phi\,\partial_\phi\right).
$$
This is the generator of <rotations> about the first coordinate axis. A natural candidate is the angular function pointing along that axis, $f_x=\sin\theta\cos\phi$. Direct substitution gives
$$
L_1f_x=i\hbar\left(\sin\phi\cos\theta\cos\phi-\cot\theta\cos\phi\sin\theta\sin\phi\right)=0.
$$
Also $f_x=(f_{+1}+f_{-1})/2$, so <linearity> and part (ii) give $L^2f_x=2\hbar^2f_x$. Thus an answer is $\sin\theta\cos\phi$. Its squared <norm> on the sphere is
$$
\int_0^{2\pi}\cos^2\phi\,d\phi\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\,d\theta=\pi\frac43=\frac{4\pi}3,
$$
so the normalized <quantum state> is
$$
\boxed{\psi_x(\theta,\phi)=\sqrt{\frac3{4\pi}}\sin\theta\cos\phi,\qquad L^2\psi_x=2\hbar^2\psi_x,\qquad L_1\psi_x=0.}
$$
The supplied starting state gives an alternative derivation with a changed <quantization axis>. Put $g=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta\sin\phi$ and $K_+=L_2+iL_3$. The <angular momentum commutation relations> give $[L_1,K_+]=\hbar K_+$ and $[L^2,K_+]=0$, so $K_+$ raises the $L_1$ projection from $-\hbar$ to zero. Direct differentiation gives
$$
L_2g=i\hbar\sin\theta\cos\phi,\qquad L_3g=\hbar\sin\theta\cos\phi,\qquad K_+g=2i\hbar\sin\theta\cos\phi,
$$
which is nonzero and proportional to the same $\psi_x$. This also connects the construction directly to the given state.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Use the normalized $L_3$ <eigenstates> from part (ii). The <quantum state> found in part (iii) has the decomposition
$$
\psi_x=\sqrt{\frac3{4\pi}}\frac{\sin\theta e^{i\phi}+\sin\theta e^{-i\phi}}2=\frac{\chi_{+1}+\chi_{-1}}{\sqrt2}.
$$
There is no $\chi_0$ component. Equivalently, its <inner product> with $\chi_0$ contains $\int_0^{2\pi}\cos\phi\,d\phi=0$. The <Born rule> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\Pr(L_3=0)=|\langle\chi_0,\psi_x\rangle|^2=0.}
$$
For completeness the only outcomes are $L_3=\pm\hbar$, each with <probability> $1/2$. Thus $\langle L_3\rangle=0$ does not mean that zero is a possible result in this state: its <quantum uncertainty> in $L_3$ is $\hbar$, even though $L_1$ is exactly zero.
